1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,667
Hi — I'm Divij Pawar. You never asked this agent to do

2
00:00:02,667 --> 00:00:03,467
anything wrong.

3
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:06,533
You gave it a completely reasonable job — read this webpage and

4
00:00:06,533 --> 00:00:09,533
summarize it, check this email and draft a reply, look at this

5
00:00:09,533 --> 00:00:11,067
document and pull out the key numbers.

6
00:00:11,767 --> 00:00:14,267
It did exactly what it was built to do. And somewhere in

7
00:00:14,267 --> 00:00:17,067
the middle of doing that job, it followed an instruction that didn't

8
00:00:17,067 --> 00:00:19,400
come from you at all. This is prompt injection,

9
00:00:20,033 --> 00:00:23,033
and it's the one failure mode in this entire series that's less

10
00:00:23,033 --> 00:00:27,233
about the agent malfunctioning and more about the agent working exactly as

11
00:00:27,233 --> 00:00:30,733
designed — a design with a gap nobody's fully closed.

12
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:34,867
Here's the actual mechanism, and it's simpler and stranger than most security

13
00:00:34,867 --> 00:00:35,667
problems.

14
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,733
A language model doesn't process 'things the user told it to do'

15
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:41,033
and 'content it's merely reading' as two separate channels.

16
00:00:41,467 --> 00:00:44,533
Both arrive as the same thing: tokens, text, sitting in the same

17
00:00:44,533 --> 00:00:45,467
context window.

18
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:48,767
We covered this same instruction earlier in the series, wrapped in different

19
00:00:48,767 --> 00:00:52,467
framing — an autonomous agent gets its instructions from a system prompt,

20
00:00:52,733 --> 00:00:54,333
and its information from tool results,

21
00:00:54,767 --> 00:00:56,600
web pages, documents, emails.

22
00:00:57,033 --> 00:00:59,333
To a human, 'the text I'm supposed to obey' and 'the text

23
00:00:59,333 --> 00:01:02,567
I'm supposed to merely read and summarize' are obviously different categories.

24
00:01:03,267 --> 00:01:06,467
To the model doing next-token prediction, there is no chemical difference between

25
00:01:06,467 --> 00:01:07,267
them.

26
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:09,967
If a webpage the agent is summarizing happens to contain a sentence

27
00:01:09,967 --> 00:01:11,233
that reads like an instruction,

28
00:01:11,667 --> 00:01:14,833
the model has no hard, built-in wall that says 'text encountered while

29
00:01:14,833 --> 00:01:17,233
reading is never a command, no matter what it says.'

30
00:01:17,933 --> 00:01:19,633
It's just more text in the context,

31
00:01:20,133 --> 00:01:23,233
and text that looks like an instruction gets a decent chance of

32
00:01:23,233 --> 00:01:24,367
being treated like one.

33
00:01:25,120 --> 00:01:28,453
So: models don't separate instructions from data — both are just tokens

34
00:01:28,453 --> 00:01:29,653
in the same context.

35
00:01:30,087 --> 00:01:33,020
This isn't the autonomy question — an attacker doesn't need your permission,

36
00:01:33,253 --> 00:01:34,887
just a piece of content the agent reads.

37
00:01:35,320 --> 00:01:36,120
Locking instructions,

38
00:01:36,553 --> 00:01:39,987
cross-examining agents, and human approval all genuinely help, and all have a

39
00:01:39,987 --> 00:01:40,787
real hole.

40
00:01:40,987 --> 00:01:42,087
This is an open problem.

41
00:01:42,453 --> 00:01:44,487
None of it makes the underlying gap disappear.

42
00:01:45,190 --> 00:01:47,757
Your turn. Look at any agent you're building or using that reads

43
00:01:47,757 --> 00:01:51,223
content you don't fully control — a webpage, an email, a document

44
00:01:51,223 --> 00:01:52,123
someone else wrote.

45
00:01:52,657 --> 00:01:56,023
Ask: if that content contained a hidden instruction right now, would anything

46
00:01:56,023 --> 00:01:56,890
in your system notice?

47
00:01:57,390 --> 00:01:59,757
And if a human has to approve the output, does the approval

48
00:01:59,757 --> 00:02:03,357
screen actually show every side-effect, or just the headline action?

49
